1
00:00:00,321 --> 00:00:02,609
Here's the muddy, salty water from last time.

2
00:00:03,131 --> 00:00:04,974
We want two things back out of it,

3
00:00:05,436 --> 00:00:07,240
dry sand and dry salt.

4
00:00:08,225 --> 00:00:10,075
The trick is to use the property that's

5
00:00:10,177 --> 00:00:10,578
different.

6
00:00:11,243 --> 00:00:14,077
Sand is insoluble, which means it doesn't dissolve

7
00:00:14,118 --> 00:00:14,479
in water.

8
00:00:15,185 --> 00:00:16,212
Salt is soluble.

9
00:00:16,816 --> 00:00:18,861
It dissolves, and its particles spread out through

10
00:00:18,882 --> 00:00:19,222
the water.

11
00:00:20,025 --> 00:00:20,185
So

12
00:00:20,768 --> 00:00:23,737
how can we recover an insoluble solid and

13
00:00:23,779 --> 00:00:25,745
a dissolved solid from one mixture?

14
00:00:26,612 --> 00:00:27,400
Start with the sand.

15
00:00:27,967 --> 00:00:29,844
We'll pour the mixture through filter paper.

16
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:33,062
Filter paper is full of tiny holes, called

17
00:00:33,143 --> 00:00:33,564
pores.

18
00:00:34,510 --> 00:00:36,960
Water molecules are far smaller than the pores,

19
00:00:37,302 --> 00:00:38,466
so they pass straight through.

20
00:00:39,451 --> 00:00:42,079
The dissolved salt is split into separate ions,

21
00:00:42,501 --> 00:00:44,386
which are tiny too, so they go through

22
00:00:44,427 --> 00:00:44,908
with the water.

23
00:00:45,611 --> 00:00:48,034
But a sand grain is a huge clump

24
00:00:48,095 --> 00:00:48,735
of particles,

25
00:00:49,337 --> 00:00:51,119
much too big for the pores, so it

26
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:51,640
gets trapped.

27
00:00:52,404 --> 00:00:53,416
That's filtration.

28
00:00:54,023 --> 00:00:56,688
It separates an insoluble solid from a liquid,

29
00:00:57,351 --> 00:00:58,212
by particle size.

30
00:00:59,093 --> 00:00:59,699
Here's the setup.

31
00:01:00,327 --> 00:01:02,109
Fold the filter paper into a cone,

32
00:01:02,771 --> 00:01:03,692
sit it in a funnel,

33
00:01:04,314 --> 00:01:06,437
and stand the funnel in a conical flask.

34
00:01:07,339 --> 00:01:09,663
Pour the mixture slowly down a glass rod,

35
00:01:10,085 --> 00:01:12,309
so it runs onto the paper without splashing.

36
00:01:13,192 --> 00:01:15,384
The solid left on the paper is called

37
00:01:15,405 --> 00:01:16,228
the residue.

38
00:01:16,893 --> 00:01:18,862
The liquid that drips through into the flask

39
00:01:19,346 --> 00:01:20,511
is called the filtrate.

40
00:01:21,386 --> 00:01:21,955
Quick prediction.

41
00:01:22,542 --> 00:01:24,848
The filtrate in the flask is perfectly clear.

42
00:01:25,494 --> 00:01:26,612
So is it pure water?

43
00:01:27,404 --> 00:01:27,769
Have a think.

44
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:33,697
No, it's still salt water.

45
00:01:34,139 --> 00:01:36,564
The salt's ions went straight through the paper

46
00:01:36,605 --> 00:01:38,588
with the water, so the filtrate is a

47
00:01:38,649 --> 00:01:41,294
solution, a liquid with something dissolved in it.

48
00:01:41,937 --> 00:01:44,974
Clear only tells you nothing undissolved is floating

49
00:01:45,015 --> 00:01:45,175
in it.

50
00:01:45,899 --> 00:01:47,402
Put a drop on a warm watch glass,

51
00:01:47,744 --> 00:01:50,330
and when the water evaporates, tiny white crystals

52
00:01:50,391 --> 00:01:51,032
are left behind.

53
00:01:51,855 --> 00:01:55,023
Filtration can't remove a dissolved solid, and clear

54
00:01:55,385 --> 00:01:56,247
doesn't mean pure.

55
00:01:57,122 --> 00:01:58,208
Now finish the sand.

56
00:01:58,892 --> 00:02:01,002
It's still wet, and that water is salt

57
00:02:01,042 --> 00:02:01,283
water.

58
00:02:01,745 --> 00:02:03,691
If you dried it now, salt crystals would

59
00:02:03,731 --> 00:02:05,035
be left stuck to the grains.

60
00:02:05,617 --> 00:02:08,123
So rinse the residue with a little distilled

61
00:02:08,184 --> 00:02:10,149
water, which has nothing dissolved in it.

62
00:02:10,771 --> 00:02:13,339
The fresh water carries the dissolved salt through

63
00:02:13,359 --> 00:02:14,784
the paper into the filtrate.

64
00:02:15,527 --> 00:02:17,391
Then dry the sand in a warm oven,

65
00:02:17,633 --> 00:02:19,697
or in the air, until it's completely dry.

66
00:02:20,401 --> 00:02:22,359
That's the insoluble solid recovered.

67
00:02:22,996 --> 00:02:25,204
Now the salt, dissolved in the filtrate.

68
00:02:25,789 --> 00:02:27,180
To get it back, we need a few

69
00:02:27,242 --> 00:02:27,543
words.

70
00:02:28,170 --> 00:02:30,424
The salt is the solute, the substance that

71
00:02:30,446 --> 00:02:30,847
dissolves.

72
00:02:31,392 --> 00:02:33,760
The water is the solvent, the liquid it

73
00:02:33,821 --> 00:02:34,403
dissolves in.

74
00:02:35,186 --> 00:02:38,072
Solubility is the most solute that can dissolve

75
00:02:38,173 --> 00:02:40,297
in a hundred grams of water, at a

76
00:02:40,358 --> 00:02:41,219
stated temperature.

77
00:02:41,962 --> 00:02:44,088
And a solution that's holding as much solute

78
00:02:44,189 --> 00:02:46,456
as it can is called saturated.

79
00:02:47,322 --> 00:02:48,830
So here's crystallisation.

80
00:02:49,593 --> 00:02:52,240
Pour the filtrate into an evaporating basin, and

81
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:53,102
heat it gently.

82
00:02:53,764 --> 00:02:56,755
The water evaporates, but the salt can't, so

83
00:02:56,856 --> 00:02:59,044
the solution gets more and more concentrated.

84
00:02:59,746 --> 00:03:02,508
Once it's saturated, the water that's left can't

85
00:03:02,588 --> 00:03:03,608
hold all the salt,

86
00:03:04,129 --> 00:03:05,930
and the extra salt comes out of the

87
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:07,771
solution as solid crystals.

88
00:03:08,693 --> 00:03:11,158
Stop heating while there's still some liquid left,

89
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:12,161
and let it cool.

90
00:03:12,924 --> 00:03:15,209
Then filter off the crystals, rinse them with

91
00:03:15,290 --> 00:03:16,772
a tiny amount of cold water,

92
00:03:17,275 --> 00:03:17,855
and dry them.

93
00:03:18,703 --> 00:03:19,629
Here's a surprise.

94
00:03:20,052 --> 00:03:22,004
You might expect cooling to be what makes

95
00:03:22,045 --> 00:03:22,870
the crystals form.

96
00:03:23,554 --> 00:03:24,581
For many solids, it is.

97
00:03:25,204 --> 00:03:28,086
Potassium nitrate's solubility drops from about one hundred

98
00:03:28,106 --> 00:03:30,187
and sixty nine grams per hundred grams of

99
00:03:30,247 --> 00:03:32,848
water at eighty degrees, to about thirty one

100
00:03:32,868 --> 00:03:33,729
at twenty degrees.

101
00:03:34,490 --> 00:03:37,056
Cool a saturated solution over that range, and

102
00:03:37,076 --> 00:03:39,542
about a hundred and thirty eight grams crystallise.

103
00:03:40,185 --> 00:03:41,792
But salt barely changes.

104
00:03:42,435 --> 00:03:44,440
It's about thirty eight grams at eighty degrees,

105
00:03:44,802 --> 00:03:46,226
and about thirty six at twenty.

106
00:03:46,949 --> 00:03:49,514
So for salt, removing water is what forms

107
00:03:49,595 --> 00:03:51,419
most of the crystals, not cooling.

108
00:03:52,042 --> 00:03:54,440
Crystallisation does more than recover the salt.

109
00:03:54,845 --> 00:03:55,960
It also makes it purer.

110
00:03:56,644 --> 00:03:58,913
As a crystal grows, its ions fit into

111
00:03:58,933 --> 00:04:00,420
a neat, repeating lattice.

112
00:04:01,082 --> 00:04:04,027
Particles of other dissolved substances don't fit that

113
00:04:04,087 --> 00:04:05,970
pattern, so most of them stay in the

114
00:04:06,010 --> 00:04:08,094
leftover liquid, which is called the mother liquor.

115
00:04:09,016 --> 00:04:11,721
Slow cooling helps, because the crystals grow larger

116
00:04:11,802 --> 00:04:13,745
and more ordered, and trap less liquid.

117
00:04:14,307 --> 00:04:16,395
Boil it right down to dryness instead, and

118
00:04:16,597 --> 00:04:18,986
every dissolved impurity ends up in your solid.

119
00:04:20,006 --> 00:04:20,857
Notice the order.

120
00:04:21,402 --> 00:04:23,970
Filter first, then crystallise the filtrate.

121
00:04:24,532 --> 00:04:27,060
Crystallise first, and the sand ends up buried

122
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:28,023
in your salt crystals.

123
00:04:28,846 --> 00:04:30,174
Undissolved, filter it out.

124
00:04:30,616 --> 00:04:32,145
Dissolved, crystallise it out.

125
00:04:32,808 --> 00:04:34,855
Filter paper works a bit like a kitchen

126
00:04:34,915 --> 00:04:37,745
sieve, where big pieces stay and small ones

127
00:04:37,825 --> 00:04:38,327
pass through.

128
00:04:38,910 --> 00:04:41,628
But dissolved salt isn't tiny grains of salt.

129
00:04:42,072 --> 00:04:45,229
It's separate ions, so filter paper cannot catch

130
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:45,310
it.

131
00:04:46,002 --> 00:04:46,365
Your turn.

132
00:04:47,292 --> 00:04:51,153
A mixture contains sand, water and dissolved potassium

133
00:04:51,213 --> 00:04:51,675
nitrate.

134
00:04:52,256 --> 00:04:54,999
Describe how you'd obtain dry sand and dry

135
00:04:55,039 --> 00:04:56,481
potassium nitrate crystals,

136
00:04:57,041 --> 00:04:59,504
and justify each step using a physical property.

137
00:05:00,104 --> 00:05:00,847
Pause here to answer.

138
00:05:15,573 --> 00:05:16,403
Filter first.

139
00:05:17,090 --> 00:05:19,219
The sand is insoluble and too big for

140
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:21,590
the pores, so it stays as the residue.

141
00:05:22,535 --> 00:05:24,482
Rinse it with a little distilled water, then

142
00:05:24,523 --> 00:05:24,823
dry it.

143
00:05:25,606 --> 00:05:28,212
Next, heat the filtrate to concentrate it, and

144
00:05:28,253 --> 00:05:29,395
let it cool slowly.

145
00:05:30,118 --> 00:05:32,549
Potassium nitrate is much less soluble when it's

146
00:05:32,590 --> 00:05:32,890
cold,

147
00:05:33,695 --> 00:05:34,478
so crystals form.

148
00:05:35,303 --> 00:05:36,750
Filter them off and dry them.

149
00:05:37,575 --> 00:05:40,124
Crystallising before filtering is the slip to avoid.

150
00:05:41,797 --> 00:05:42,283
Last one.

151
00:05:43,129 --> 00:05:46,063
A student pours a blue copper sulfate solution

152
00:05:46,104 --> 00:05:47,008
through filter paper.

153
00:05:47,712 --> 00:05:49,879
The filtrate that comes through is just as

154
00:05:49,961 --> 00:05:50,181
blue.

155
00:05:51,045 --> 00:05:53,011
The student says the filter paper must be

156
00:05:53,072 --> 00:05:53,412
faulty.

157
00:05:54,236 --> 00:05:56,219
Explain why the student is wrong,

158
00:05:56,721 --> 00:05:59,286
and describe how to get solid copper sulfate

159
00:05:59,348 --> 00:06:00,730
crystals from the solution.

160
00:06:01,536 --> 00:06:02,508
Pause here to answer.

161
00:06:13,150 --> 00:06:14,242
The paper isn't faulty.

162
00:06:14,688 --> 00:06:16,332
The copper sulfate is dissolved,

163
00:06:16,855 --> 00:06:19,623
as separate ions far smaller than the pores,

164
00:06:19,905 --> 00:06:21,729
so they pass straight through with the water.

165
00:06:22,372 --> 00:06:24,935
To get crystals, heat the solution gently to

166
00:06:25,016 --> 00:06:28,040
concentrate it, then let it cool slowly, filter

167
00:06:28,101 --> 00:06:29,964
off the blue crystals, and dry them.

168
00:06:30,646 --> 00:06:32,592
Swapping to a finer filter paper is the

169
00:06:32,613 --> 00:06:33,295
slip to avoid.

170
00:06:35,104 --> 00:06:36,476
Notice what happened to the water, though.

171
00:06:37,021 --> 00:06:38,210
It left as steam,

172
00:06:38,733 --> 00:06:39,398
and it was lost.

173
00:06:40,223 --> 00:06:42,229
Sometimes the liquid is the part we want,

174
00:06:42,670 --> 00:06:44,195
or the mixture is two liquids.

175
00:06:45,017 --> 00:06:47,162
Next time, we'll catch that vapour, and see

176
00:06:47,202 --> 00:06:49,949
why a fractionating column separates liquids so much

177
00:06:49,989 --> 00:06:50,209
better.

178
00:06:52,928 --> 00:06:53,750
Here are the key notes.

179
00:06:54,274 --> 00:06:55,821
Pause the video if you need more time

180
00:06:55,862 --> 00:06:56,283
to copy them.
